</tr><tr id="_e426401c-d457-4e92-5b95-012b112c6b26"><td id="_e72b250e-9d56-a46b-b310-44c8929c6e4e" valign="top" align="left"><p id="_752f20f1-3b0a-dcc5-4116-5f8f74ef301e"><underline>INT 1 Reference:</underline></p>

<clause id="_7ec56def-a66b-31f3-6390-efb046f58d10" anchor="sec_5.11.1" obligation="normative">
<title id="_b49f6126-5243-f04b-655a-970409b0c0bc">Aggregation of approximate areas in general</title>
<p id="_07b3d5fb-2143-6fc3-5941-aa73003cfa4c">A ‘fuzzy area’ will consist of a ‘core’ feature of the appropriate geographic feature type and the appropriate geometry (e.g., a <strong>RadioServiceArea</strong> area feature) and one or more ‘fuzzy’ <strong>Indeterminate Zone</strong> features (with surface geometry).</p>

<p id="_2b8d3b6f-ffd8-7864-59c2-05c93208175b">To encode fuzzy areas, cartographers must demarcate areas where the information is of uncertain applicability and associate the areas of uncertainty to the appropriate sub-type of  <strong>FuzzyAreaAggregate</strong>, along with the area feature where the information is certain (the “core feature”).</p>

<p id="_f27ddec6-f591-a3f9-204e-14aef6dafb9d"><underline>Remarks:</underline></p>

<p id="_7acbee97-8d94-b423-e02f-34570cff00ff"><underline>Distinction:</underline></p>
</clause>
</td>
<td id="_21831288-8a68-78bd-a6b3-b5c0ec273603" valign="top" align="left"/><td id="_e134207b-11dc-2bce-9dd8-e448243f32d9" valign="top" align="left"/><td id="_5fe94d31-0af5-d26c-2eb1-d2da996eff65" valign="top" align="left"/><td id="_bd78f5db-c223-55a4-8029-e680e728cffb" valign="top" align="left"/><td id="_fd4df92d-8884-a267-3eaa-4c766a7f2bb9" valign="top" align="left"/><td id="_89f3c472-05ac-d9ad-f147-d0db0b5cec38" valign="top" align="left"/></tr></tbody>
</table>

